Macedonians Vote in Refiry on Their Country's Name Change
Last Sunday the Macedonian people voted in a referendum on the possible modification of the name of their country in ‘Republic of North Macedonia’, thus relieving a dispute with Greece that has been dragging on for decades and would no longer impede the Macedonian application for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece also owns a province called Macedonia, and states that the name of its northern neighbor means that the country is entitled to Greek territory. As a result, Greece vetoped the incorporation of Macedonia into the European Union and NATO.
The two administrations concluded an agreement in June based on the proposed new name, but nationalists find that the name change would undermining the ethnic identities of the Slavic majority in Macedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he is not going to vote in the referendum and because of a boycott campaign it’s not certain that at least 50 per-percurrence will be achieved, so the referendum might not be valid.
The question on the ballot is worded as follows: ‘Are you in favour of membership of NATO and the EU with acceptance of the agreement with Greece?’
“Professors of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, find that the price to be wagered is worth trying to join authorities such as the EU and NATO. Macedonia is one of the countries that arranged when the former Yugoslavia disintegrated.
“I’m going to vote today for the country’s future, for the young people of Macedonia so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because that will lead to a safer life for all of us, said 79-year-old Oliver Georgijevska in Skopje.
Although the referendum does not be legally binding, enough parliamentarians have indicated that they will respect the result, which will make it decisive.
The name change would require a two-third-large majority in parliament.
The National Electoral Commission said that there had been no reports of irregularities by 1 p.m.
However, the turn turnout was only 16 per cent, compared to 34 per cent during the previous parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters voted.
“I’m going to vote because it’s important to my children. It’s our place in Europe, 62-year-old Gjose Tanevski, a voter in the capital Skopje.
Prime Minister Zoran Zaev of Macedonia, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko voted in Strumica, Macedonia on September 30, 2018 during the country’s recitation regarding the country's name change that would open the way for membership of NATO and the European Union.
In front of parliament in Skopje the 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov put on a small stage and placed chairs for tents that were placed by people boycotting the referendum.
"We are in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to take part as a full-fledged member, and we do not through a loophole, Kavadarkov said.
“We’re a poor country, but we have our dignity.
“If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to other countries, such as China and Russia, becoming part of Euro-Second integration.”
Premier Zaev says NATO membership will provide much-needed investment for Macedonia, an unemployment rate of more than 20 per cent.
"I'm convinced that the vast majority is in favour because more than 80 per cent of our citizens are in favour of the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
“He said a ‘yes’ as a result would be “configuration of our future.”
An opinion poll published last Monday by the Macedonian Institute of Policy Research found that between 30 and 43 per cent of the voters would take part in the referendum less than the required turn-off.
From another poll, conducted by Hot Macedonian television station Telma TV, it was concluded that 57 per cent of the respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Seventy percent would vote yes.
If the referendum is to be a success, the turn-off must be 50 percent plus one vote.
A fuunity of the referendum would be the first serious blow to pro-Western government's policies since it came to power in May last year.
