John Edward: Language Skills Essential For Global Citizens
The Independent Schools in Scotland have an excellent track record of academic excellence, and this continued in 2018 with another set of outstanding exam results, as well as individual and collective success in sports, art, music and other initiatives of the entire community.
With more than 30,000 apprentice learners in Scotland these schools, represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to deliver the best service to their students and parents.
Independent schools strive to prepare their pupils for secondary and higher education, their chosen careers and their place as global citizens.
As an educational sector that can design and implement a bespoke school curriculum, we see that modern languages stay popular and desired subjects par excellence within schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you talk to someone in a language that he understands, you reach his head.
“When you talk to him in his own language, you reach his heart.”
“This isn’t a powerful recollection that we can’t only rely on English when we want to build relationships and a relationship of trust with people from other countries.
From this year’s recent exam results we can deduce that languages are at the top of the ranking, with the highest success rates within independent schools.
In total, 68 per cent of pupils who studied foreign languages achieved a high score.
The data collected from 74 schools affiliated with SCIS shows that 72 percent of the students achieved a high score (‘A’) in Mandarin, while 72 per cent of German, 69 per cent of French students and 63 per cent of Spanish students also achieved an A.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages such as essential skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages are now valued just as much as STEM topics (science, technology, engineering and math) in independent school programs and elsewhere.
A study conducted by the UK Commission on Employment and Skills in 2014 found that the stumbling blocks to complete job listings, 17 per cent were attributed to a lack of language skills, according to employers.
Linguistic skills are therefore becoming more and more important to prepare young people for their future careers.
As more and more job opportunities require language proficiency, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
No matter the career that a person chooses, if he has learned a second language, in the future he will have a real advantage with such a skill for life.
Communicating directly with people from abroad gives a multi-multiling-to-biking self-evident competitive advantage.
According to a YouGov poll of more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75 percent were unable to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation. French was the only language that exceeded 10 per cent, totaling 15 per cent.
That’s the reason why investing in language education is now important to today’s children.
Being able to speak multiple languages, especially those of emerging economies, will provide children with a better chance of finding meaningful jobs.
In Scotland, schools will choose which languages to inecite.
A number of schools will focus on the more classic modern languages, while other schools will teach languages that are most important to the United Kingdom when looking ahead to 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
No matter your child's interest, there will always be several languages to choose from in independent schools, with teachers specializing in this field.
“Scottish independent schools are committed to providing a learning environment that prepares children and gives them the skills they need to succeed no matter the future.”
At the moment it cannot be denominated that languages in a global business environment continue to be of vital importance for the future of the country, so this should be reflected in education.
“Modern languages should actually be considered “International Communication Skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to young people in Scotland.
- Il faut bien le faire.
John lyzes Director of the Scottish Council of Inseversive Schools
