Almanac: The Inventor of the Geiger Counter
September 30, 1882, exactly 1–136 years ago, a page from the almanak of our 'Sunday Morning': 30 September 1882, exactly 136 years ago, for those who can play... the day on which future physicist Johannes Wilhelm 'Hans' Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method of detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the device called geigerteller.
The geigerteller has not been thought away from science ever since, and also became an icon in pop culture, e.g. in ‘Bells or Coronado’, a film from 1950 depicting improbable cowboy-scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans at first sight:
"Which for God's sake?"
Rogers: ‘That’s a gefinder counter that’s used to detect radioactive minerals, such as uric uranium.
If you put these headphones, you will hear the effect of the atoms evoked by radioactivity in the minerals.”
Evan’s: “It’s really going to bang now!”
'Hans' Geiger died in 1945, barely a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name remains relevant.
