The Inventor of the World Wide Web Plans a New Internet To Tack For Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web, starts a start-up that wants to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt, the latest technology legend project, is a company that is based on Berners-Lee’s open-source Solid platform.
Solid offers users the ability to choose where their data is stored and which people can access what information.
“In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that ‘world dominance’ is the guiding thought behind Inrupt.
"We have to do this now," he said about the start-up.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The app utilizes Solid’s technology to allow people to make their own ‘personal online data storage’ or POD.
Lists of contacts, checklists of activity lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools can be saved.
It's as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available together through one browser and all at the same time.
What makes POD unique is the fact that it depends entirely on the user who has access to what information.
The company calls this "personal empowerment through data".
According to CEO John Bruce, the idea behind Inrupt is that the company provides the company with the resources, processes and necessary skills to make Solid available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform that was purchased by IBM, some developers who have been contracted to work on the project, and a community of volunteer programmers.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized apps with the tools available on Inrupt’s website.
Berners-Lee said that he and his team won't have conversations with "Facebook and Google about whether or not to introduce a full-fledged transformation so that all of their business models are completely upside down at once".
“We don’t ask for their permission.”
In a post on Medium published on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that the “mission (van Inrupt) is to deliver the commercial energy and an ecosystem that helps protect the integrity and quality of the new web, built on Solid”.
In 1994 Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the net neutrality debate.
Even after Inrupt’s inception, Berners-Lee will remain as founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about this new era for the web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: clergyman and porter of the Victoria Cross from World War I honoured
