Germany's new warship postponed
German navy’s newest frigate was supposed to have come into service in 2014, replacing the obsolete warships of the Cold War time, but it will be at least one more year before it’s completed due to faulty systems and rising costs, local media reports.
The commissioning of the 'Rheinland-Pfalz', the leading ship in the brand-new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the newspaper Die Zeit which quotes a military spokesman.
The vessel was supposed to be part of the navy in 2014, the trodgeable issues after delivery have plagued the fate of the ambitious project.
The four vessels of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class ordered the navy in 2007 serve to replace the obsolete frigates of Bremen-class.
As far as we all know they have a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft missiles and a form of stealth technology, such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic characteristics.
Other important features are longer maintenance cycles, it should be possible to deploy the latest frigates up to two years in a row, far away from home ports.
However, the ongoing delays mean that the advanced warships, which are intended to give Germany the opportunity to project its power overseas, are already obsolete by the time they are put into operation, Die Zeit notes.
Disastrous frigate F125 already made headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to put the vessel into operation and sent it back to the Blohm & Vos shipyard in Hamburg.
This was the first time the navy had returned a ship to a shipbuilder after delivery.
There is little known about the reasons behind this return, but German media cited some crucial “professive software and hardware failures” that would necessitate the warship if deployed in a combat mission.
Software defects were especially important because Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels are serviced by a crew of about 120 sailors, half man-power on the frigates of the elder Bremen-class.
It also became clear that the ship suffers from a serious over-heavy, which will reduce its performance and the capabilities of the navy is limited to carry out further upgrades.
The 'Rheinland-Pfalz weighs 7,000 tons, which is twice as much as ships of a similar class deployed by the Germans during the Second World War.
In addition to the defective hardware, the price tag for the entire project, including crew training, will also become a problem.
It’s been said to have reached dizzuishing €3.1 billion, compared to the original €2.2 billion.
The problems that the latest frigates have in their grip will become especially important due to the recent warnings that German navy’s power is diminishing.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, Head of the Military Defense Commission in Germany, acknowledged that the navy is actually "a shortage of employable ships".
The official said that the problem over time has grown like a snowball, because old ships were taken out of operation but no replacement vessels were delivered.
"He complained that none of the frigates of the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class were yet available to be incorporated into the navy.
National Trust Releasing The Secret Life of Bats
New research being carried out on an estate in the Scottish highlands tries to reveal how bats make use of the landscape in their quest for food.
It is hoped that the findings will shed new light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and will contribute to future conservation activities.
The study carried out by scientists from the National Trust for Scotland follows the common and small dwarf bats, the brown Grand Orley Bat and the water bat at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Dedicated recording equipment will be placed at key locations around the estate to monitor the activities of the bats throughout the season.
Employees of the NTS and volunteers will also carry out mobile studies using hand detectors.
Despicious sound analysis of all recordings will determine the frequency of lubritothe cries of the wreaket and what kind does.
Subsequently a map of the habitat and a report is produced in order to create a detailed landscape-scale image of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, Nature Conservation Adviser of the NTS, hopes that the results will show which areas of the habitat are the most important for bats and how they are used by the species.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management, such as grazing fields and how to best maintain forests for bats and similar species.
Malmuth populations in Scotland and the rest of the U.K. have declined significantly over the past century.
They’re threatened by construction and development activities that affect places of berths and the loss of habitat.
Wind turbines and lightning can also pose a risk just like fly catchers and certain chemical treatments of building materials and attacks by domestic cats.
Bats aren’t really blind.
Because of their nocturnal hunting habits, however, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to catching prey.
They use an advanced ultrasound-location technique to determine the position of insects and obstacles in their flight route.
The NTS, responsible for care for more than 270 historical buildings, 38 major gardens and 76,000 hectares of land in the country, bats are taken very seriously.
The NTS possesses ten trained experts, who regularly carry out inspections, inspections of sleeping places and sometimes rescue operations.
In fact, the organization has set up the first and only curles reserve for bats in Scotland at Threave Estate in Dumfries and Galloway, where eight of Scotland’s ten species of bats live.
Landscape manager David Thompson says that the estate is the ideal territory for the bats.
"Here in Threave we've got an amazing setting for bats," he said.
"We have the old buildings, lots of very old trees and the right habitat.
“But there’s so much known about bats, and that’s why the work we’re doing here and at other locations will help us better understand what they need to thrive.”
He emphasizes the importance of checking bats before maintenance work is carried out, because the potentially unconscious destruction of a berth can result in the deaths of 400 female bats and their young, potentially eradicating an entire local population.
Bats are a protected species and it is illegal to kill, harass or disturb them or to destroy their berths.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scottish official for the Bat Conservation Trust, has prompted the public to make a contribution to help.
She said: "We still need to learn a lot about our bats, and from many of our straits we simply don't even know how their populations are doing."
